// Practice quiz: Pass-by-value
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT reserves braces and equals signs, so array contents and assignments are escaped or written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/pass-by-value

::o1 ignored result::A method static int twice(int n) runs n \= n * 2 and then returns n. main sets int v \= 4, calls twice(v) without using the result, and prints v. What prints? {
=4#Correct. The call copies 4 into n; doubling n changes only that copy, and the returned 8 is thrown away.
~8#Not quite. v would be 8 only if main stored the result, as in v \= twice(v).
~0#Not quite. Nothing resets v; it keeps the value 4 it had before the call.
~A compile error, because the result of twice is ignored#Not quite. Java allows a call to ignore a returned value.
}

::o1 getting a new value back::A method should give the caller a new int value. Which approach works? {
=Return the new value and have the caller assign it, as in v \= twice(v)#Correct. The parameter is only a copy, so the new value must come back through return.
~Reassign the parameter inside the method#Not quite. That changes only the method's own copy in its frame.
~Give the parameter the same name as the caller's variable#Not quite. Matching names does not connect the two variables; each frame has its own.
~Declare the parameter final#Not quite. final stops the parameter from being reassigned; it does not reach the caller.
}

::o2 uppercase inside a method::A method static void shout(String s) runs s \= s.toUpperCase(). main sets String word \= "hi", calls shout(word), and prints word. What prints? {
=hi#Correct. toUpperCase makes a new String, and the assignment points only shout's copy s at it; word still refers to "hi".
~HI#Not quite. word would change only if main assigned a new String to word itself.
~null#Not quite. Nothing assigns null to word; it still refers to its original String.
~A compile error, because a String parameter cannot be reassigned#Not quite. A parameter is an ordinary local variable and may be reassigned.
}

::o2 why the text stays::Why can a method not change the text of the String its caller passed in? {
=String objects are constant, and reassigning the parameter only redirects the method's own copy of the reference#Correct. No code can edit a String's text, and the caller's variable keeps its own reference.
~Java passes Strings by reference but locks them#Not quite. Java passes every argument by value; for a String the value copied is a reference.
~String parameters are automatically final#Not quite. A String parameter can be reassigned; the caller just does not see it.
~Methods cannot receive String arguments#Not quite. Methods take String parameters all the time.
}

::o3 change through the reference::A method static void fill(int[] a) runs a[1] \= 9. main creates nums holding 1, 2 and 3, calls fill(nums), and prints nums[1]. What prints? {
=9#Correct. The copied value is a reference to the same array, so a change made through a is visible through nums.
~2#Not quite. That would be true for a primitive, but here both variables refer to one array.
~1#Not quite. nums[1] is the second element, which fill changed.
~A compile error, because arrays cannot be parameters#Not quite. Arrays can be passed like any other object.
}

::o3 how arrays are passed::Which statement best describes how Java passes an array argument? {
=It copies the reference into the parameter, so both variables refer to the same array#Correct. Java passes every argument by value, and for an object the value is the reference.
~It passes the variable by reference, so reassigning the parameter also changes the caller's variable#Not quite. Reassigning the parameter only redirects the method's copy of the reference.
~It copies the whole array, so changes never reach the caller#Not quite. Only the reference is copied; the array itself is shared.
~It passes arrays by value, so no change inside the method is ever visible#Not quite. Changes made through the shared reference, such as a[1] \= 9, are visible.
}
